      "The second tells an AI exactly what you do, where, and how quickly. It's quotable. The first says nothing it can use. Service pages and FAQs written in this direct style give assistants material to work with.",
      "### 4. Their reviews say specific things",
      "Star ratings and review counts matter, but so does what reviewers write. A review that says 'They installed our new sump pump in Woodbridge the same day our old one failed' connects your business to a service, a place and an outcome. Hundreds of reviews like that make it easy for an assistant to match you to a specific request. Our post on [Google reviews in the age of AI search](/blog/google-reviews-and-ai-search) covers how to earn them.",
      "### 5. Their website is technically readable",
      "If your key information is buried in images, hidden behind scripts that crawlers struggle with, or on pages that load slowly, AI tools may never see it. A fast site with clean HTML, clear headings and structured data makes your information easy to process. It also matters that your site doesn't accidentally block the crawlers AI tools use. See [technical SEO basics](/blog/technical-seo-basics).",
      "## How each assistant differs",
      "The details shift often, but a few broad patterns are worth knowing:",
      "- **Google's AI Overviews and AI Mode** draw on Google's own search index and Business Profile data, so your Google presence matters most here. See [Google AI Overviews explained](/blog/google-ai-overviews-explained).",
